--TEST--
Sanitizing a `Core\Html::sanitize` answer again changes nothing, over the documents whose parse differs from their source — `rule:core-classes/html-sanitize`, `rule:core-classes/html-parsing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every payload below reads one way to a scanner over the text and another way
// to the parser a browser runs: the content of a raw-text element reparses as
// markup, a comment closes early, an attribute value closes its own quote. A
// sanitizer that read the source and deleted from it would approve a document
// it never saw, which is the whole of the mutation-XSS class. Rebuilding from
// the parsed tree is what closes it — `rule:core-classes/html-sanitize`.
// Asked of the *answer's own parse* rather than of its text, because that is
// the question: a handler is dangerous when it is an attribute a browser
// builds, and the same characters sitting in element content or inside a
// granted `title` are prose. `Core\Html::parse` is the same door the sanitizer
// itself went through, so this walks what a browser would build.
class Handlers {
    public static function count(Core\Xml\Node $node): int {
        int $found = 0;
        if ($node->kind() == Core\Xml\NodeKind::Element) {
            foreach ($node->attributes() as tainted string $name => tainted string $value) {
                if (Core\Str::startsWith($name, "on")) {
                    $found = $found + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        foreach ($node->children() as Core\Xml\Node $child) {
            $found = $found + Handlers::count($child);
        }
        return $found;
    }
}

array<string> $mutating = [
    "<listing>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</listing>",
    "<xmp><p>&lt;/xmp&gt;&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</p></xmp>",
    "<noscript><p title=\"</noscript><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\">",
    "<style><img src=x onerror=alert(1)></style>",
    "<!--><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>-->",
    "<a href=\"java\tscript:alert(1)\">x</a>",
    "<p title=\"&quot;><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\">t</p>",
];

// Counted rather than read off a line, because what is under test is a
// property of the whole set: a member that reaches a fixed point on six of
// seven has the bug this case exists for, and printing the six would look
// right.
int $fixed = 0;
int $inert = 0;
foreach ($mutating as string $payload) {
    string $once = Core\Html::toSource(
        Core\Html::sanitize($payload),
        "comparing an answer against the answer's own answer"
    );
    string $twice = Core\Html::toSource(
        Core\Html::sanitize($once),
        "the second pass, which is the one that must change nothing"
    );
    if ($once == $twice) {
        $fixed = $fixed + 1;
    }
    // The fixed point is the interesting half, but it would also be reached by
    // a member that answered its argument unchanged. What makes it mean
    // something is that nothing a browser builds out of the answer carries a
    // handler. Some of these answers *do* still say `onerror` — as escaped
    // characters in element content, or inside a `title` the list does grant —
    // and that is the right answer rather than a near miss: what a document
    // says is its own business, and what it may run is this member's.
    if (Handlers::count(Core\Html::parse($once)) == 0) {
        $inert = $inert + 1;
    }
}

echo "fixed ", $fixed, " of ", Core\Arr::count($mutating), "\n";
echo "no handler ", $inert, " of ", Core\Arr::count($mutating), "\n";
--EXPECT--
fixed 7 of 7
no handler 7 of 7
